orcing the importance of considering sex differences in future studies related to steroid metabolism and cardiovascular health."; Example 3: "This study investigates the emergence of glufosinate-resistant rigid ryegrass (Lolium rigidum) in Greece, responding to recent reports from growers regarding control failures associated with this herbicide. Glufosinate, a broad-spectrum non-selective herbicide, has been widely used for weed management; however, repetitive applications can increase selection pressure, leading to herbicide-resistant weed populations. The objectives of the study included confirming the presence of glufosinate-resistant L. rigidum and assessing how different growth stages of this weed affect glufosinate efficacy. Researchers sampled twenty populations of rigid ryegrass from five regions across Greece and performed whole plant dose\u2013response tests on five populations under controlled conditions. They applied eight different rates of glufosinate (ranging from 0.0 to 6.0 kg a.i. ha\u22121) to evaluate its effectiveness on the sampled populations. Findings confirmed glufosinate resistance in three of the five populations studied, with resistance levels varying from three to seven times greater than those in susceptible populations, as measured by above-ground biomass reduction. Additionally, the efficacy of glufosinate against rigid ryegrass was found to be influenced by the growth stage of the plant at the time of application. These results highlight the need for ongoing monitoring of herbicide resistance and the importance of exploring alternative weed management strategies to ensure the effectiveness of glufosinate in agricultural practices”. 
